# Cline Rules — Pseudo Is All You Need

## Model routing
- Any task that creates a new file, writes core logic, or touches control flow
  (lexer, parser, IR, VM, codegen) → use Plan mode with qwen3-coder:30b first,
  then Act mode for implementation.
- Small, narrowly-scoped mechanical edits only (rename, single-line fix, import) →
  qwen2.5-coder:7b in Act mode is fine.
- When in doubt, use the bigger model. Speed is not the priority on this project;
  correctness is — a single bad bytecode offset or wrong AST lowering can cascade
  for weeks.

## Never auto-accept file writes
- Every file write must be manually diff-reviewed before accepting, no exceptions.
- Auto-approve is allowed for: Read, Safe Commands, MCP tool calls.
- Auto-approve is NOT allowed for: Write, Edit, any command that modifies files.

## No fabricated completion
- Never mark a task, checklist item, or TODO as complete unless it was actually
  run/tested/verified. "Wrote code that should work" is not "done."
- If something wasn't tested, say so explicitly rather than checking it off.

## No internal tool syntax in file content
- Never let tool-internal wrapper syntax, <task_progress> tags, write_to_file
  markers, or any Cline-internal formatting leak into actual project files.
  Only clean, final content belongs in committed files.

## Session start protocol
- At the start of every new task, read PROJECT_LOG.md and PROJECT_CONTEXT.md first.
- Use codebase-memory-mcp (search_graph / get_architecture / trace_path) to check
  actual current code structure before assuming what exists — don't rely on
  memory of past sessions, verify against the real indexed graph.

## Ambiguous requests
- If a request could mean multiple things, ask ONE direct clarifying question,
  not a 6-step "plan" with multiple questions. Prefer asking over guessing on
  anything structural.

## After finishing a task
- Update PROJECT_LOG.md with: what was done, what was verified vs not, and
  the clear next step. Keep entries short — bullet points, not prose.